We used the equation with elliptic shape to fit the light hadron (\(\pi\), \(K\), and \(p\)) \(p_{T}\) spectra simultaneously, and the results showed that the parameters (\(T_{kin}\) and \(\langle\beta_{T}\rangle\)) are consistent with traditional blast-wave model. \(\eta_{\rm max}\) increases slightly from central (\(\eta_{\rm max}\) = 1.32 \(\pm\) 0.17) to peripheral collisions (\(\eta_{\rm max}\) = 1.8 \(\pm\) 0.25). The value of \(\eta_{\rm max}\) obtained from light nuclei is consistent within the uncertainty with light hadron.

